dropped the body
--
N5008 [temp.mem]/1 + [temp.over.link]: each out-of-class member definition
belongs to the member with the same signature -- `function<R(A...)>::
function(const function &)' to the copy constructor, `template <class F>
function<R(A...)>::function(F)' to the converting constructor template.
The front end attached the copy constructor's out-of-line definition to
the constructor template as well (its `other.f' is then checked with
`other' a function pointer: "member operator requires struct/union type on
left hand side"), dropped the template's body and the constructed object
was nondet. The attach paths now prefer a definition with as many template
parameters as the class (an ordinary member) over one with more (a member
template), in either order. This is libc++'s std::function (function.h:852
declares `template <class _Fp, class> function(_Fp)'; the copy
constructor's definition at line 919 precedes the template's at 938),
dropped in cpp11_function_basic_libcxx and cpp17_functional_basic_libcxx
(round-163 audit). Header-free. Fails on bin53.
